---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import Command from '../components/Command.astro'
import Loupe from '../components/Loupe.astro'
import MeasuredHeading from '../components/MeasuredHeading.astro'
import { steps } from '../lib/steps'
import { specimen } from '../lib/specimen'
import { results } from '../lib/results'
import { blob, install, site } from '../lib/site'
---

<Base description={site.description}>
  <section class="page-head hero">
    <MeasuredHeading>A design review that shows its measurements.</MeasuredHeading>
    <p class="lede">
      loupe is a Claude Code skill. It reviews an interface against Apple’s Human Interface Guidelines and the research
      around them. It measures what it can in the running app, says how strongly Apple states each rule it cites, and
      tells you when a screen looks like every other template. It works on iOS, iPadOS and macOS apps and on the web.
    </p>
    <Command text={install.skills} label="install command" />
    <p class="muted">
      Run it in a terminal. Free and MIT-licensed. <a href="#install">Other ways to install, including the Claude Code plugin</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="specimen-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="specimen-h">{specimen.spots.length} findings from a blind run</h2>
      <p>
        On a tool-lending library page, written for our tests by a separate model that was told not to read loupe’s
        rules. Pick a finding to move the ring to it.
      </p>
    </header>
    <Loupe />
    <p><a href="/cases/lendwell">The whole report, next to the review written without loupe</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="tags-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="tags-h">Every finding has two tags</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="sheet-grid">
      <div class="frame">
        <div class="frame-label"><span>Impact</span><span>harm to the user</span></div>
        <dl class="tag-defs">
          <dt><span class="tag blocker">Blocker</span></dt>
          <dd>
            Needs evidence of one of these: a measured or source-confirmed WCAG A or AA failure; an Apple MUST on an
            iPhone, iPad or Mac app whose breach stops the task, blocks an accessibility need or risks App Review
            rejection; data loss; deceptive design; or a task that can’t be finished in a release build.
          </dd>
          <dt><span class="tag">Major</span></dt>
          <dd>Feels wrong, non-native or generic. Judged issues stop here.</dd>
          <dt><span class="tag">Minor</span> <span class="tag">Polish</span></dt>
          <dd>Small costs, and finish.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div class="frame">
        <div class="frame-label"><span>Apple column</span><span>how strongly it is stated</span></div>
        <dl class="tag-defs">
          <dt><span class="tag">MUST</span> <span class="tag">SHOULD</span> <span class="tag">CONSIDER</span></dt>
          <dd>Copied from Apple’s wording. Never upgraded.</dd>
          <dt><span class="tag">Web</span></dt>
          <dd>An Apple rule translated to the web, or a WCAG-backed web rule.</dd>
          <dt><span class="tag">Research</span> <span class="tag">Taste</span></dt>
          <dd>Outside research where Apple is silent, and reviewer judgement, labelled as such.</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p><a href="/method#rules-h">The full rules, quoted from the skill</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="steps-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="steps-h">The steps of a review</h2>
      <p>Numbered as in the skill.</p>
    </header>
    <ol class="steps">
      {
        steps().map((s) => (
          <li>
            <span class="step-n">{s.n}</span>
            <h3>{s.name}</h3>
            <p>{s.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
    <p><a href="/method">What it reads and measures, and its rules</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="results-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="results-h">{results.headline}</h2>
      <p>{results.summary}</p>
    </header>
    <p><a href="/testing">Method, every pair, and the losses</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="install" aria-labelledby="install-h">
    <header>
      <h2 id="install-h">Install</h2>
    </header>
    <div class="install-list">
      <div class="field">
        <h3>Inside Claude Code, as a plugin</h3>
        <Command text={install.plugin} label="plugin install commands" quiet />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <h3>In a terminal, for any agent that reads skills</h3>
        <Command text={install.skills} label="skills CLI install command" quiet />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <h3>Clone and link, so git pull updates it</h3>
        <Command text={install.symlink} label="clone and link commands" quiet />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="muted">
      Then ask in plain words: “review the checkout flow at localhost:3000”, “does this screen look AI-made?”, “audit our
      tokens”. Measuring a web app live needs the Playwright MCP server; without it loupe works from source and
      screenshots and lists the measured checks as unverified. <a href={blob('README.md')}>README</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
